WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.680
Today we are going to talk about an amazing&nbsp;
new technique to make these beautiful, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:17.880
very expensive computer simulations cheaper. How&nbsp;
much cheaper? Well, I will tell you in a moment.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:24.780
You see, around here, we discuss research&nbsp;
papers like this and this. We talk a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.720
deal about how difficult and computationally&nbsp;
expensive it is to simulate all this physics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.080
but that’s not the only thing that needs to&nbsp;
be done here. All this data also has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:43.320
stored too! That is a ton of geometry&nbsp;
information. How much exactly? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:51.060
15 gigabytes for a simulation is not uncommon, and&nbsp;
my fluid simulation that you see here took well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.460
over a hundred gigabytes of space to store.&nbsp;
Some of the more extreme examples can fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:04.020
several hard drives for only a minute of physics&nbsp;
simulation data. That is an insane amount of data.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:11.400
So, we need something to help compressing down all&nbsp;
this data. And the weapon of choice for working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:18.600
with this kind of volumetric data is often a&nbsp;
tool called OpenVDB. It has this hierarchical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:26.640
structure within, and scientists at NVIDIA had a&nbsp;
crazy idea. They said, how about exchanging this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.100
with all of these amazing learning-based methods&nbsp;
that you are hearing about everywhere? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.280
okay, but why just throw a neural network at&nbsp;
the problem? Is this really going to help?&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:45.660
Well, maybe. You see, neural networks have&nbsp;
exceptional compression capabilities. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:52.200
instance, we have seen with their earlier paper&nbsp;
that such an AI can help us transmit video data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:02:01.200
of us by, get this: only taking the first image&nbsp;
from the video, and just a tiny bit of extra data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:11.100
and they throw away the entire video afterwards!
So, can these techniques do some more magic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.700
the area of physics simulations? Those are&nbsp;
much more complex, but who knows, let’s see!

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:24.360
And now, hold on to your papers, because it&nbsp;
looks like this. Now that is all well and good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:33.660
but here is the kicker, it takes about 95% less&nbsp;
storage than the previous method. Yes, that is 20x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:41.520
compression while the two pieces of footage still&nbsp;
look the same. Wow. That is absolutely incredible.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.920
And the previous OpenVDB solution&nbsp;
is not just for some hobby projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.320
it is used in many of the blockbuster&nbsp;
feature-length movies you all know and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.800
love. These movies won many-many academy&nbsp;
awards. And, we are not done yet. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.880
even close - it gets even better! If you&nbsp;
think 20x is the best it can do, now look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:15.240
we also have 50x here. And sometimes, it&nbsp;
gets up to even 60x smaller. My goodness!

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:22.440
And this new NeuralVDB technique from NVIDIA&nbsp;
can be used for not only these amazing movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:29.220
but for everything else OpenVDB was&nbsp;
useful for. Oh yes! That means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:36.780
we can go beyond these movies, and use it for&nbsp;
scientific and even industrial visualizations.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.720
They also have the machinery to read and&nbsp;
process all this data with a graphics card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.400
therefore it not only decreases the&nbsp;
memory footprint of these techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:55.920
but it even improves how quickly they run. My&nbsp;
mind is officially blown. So much progress in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.920
so little time. And this is my favorite kind&nbsp;
of research, and that is when we look into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.880
intersection of computer graphics and AI. That’s&nbsp;
where we find some of the most beautiful works.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.300
And don’t forget, these applications are&nbsp;
plentiful. This could also be used with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:21.480
FourCastNet, which is a physics model that&nbsp;
can predict outlier weather events. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.860
coolest part about this work is that&nbsp;
it runs not in a data center anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:32.640
but today, it runs on just one NVIDIA&nbsp;
graphics card. And with NeuralVDB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.580
it will require even less memory to do&nbsp;
so. And it will help with all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:44.580
absolute miracles of science that you see&nbsp;
here too. So, all of these could run with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:51.120
an up to 60 times smaller memory footprint?&nbsp;
That is incredible. What a time to be alive!

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:57.960
Huge congratulations to Doyub Kim, the first&nbsp;
author of the paper and his team for this amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:06.300
achievement. And once again, you see the power of&nbsp;
AI, the power of the papers, and tech transfer at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:13.260
the same time. And, since NVIDIA has an excellent&nbsp;
record in putting these tools into the hands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:19.440
all of us, for instance, they did it with the&nbsp;
previous incarnation of this technique, NanoVDB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:26.940
I am convinced that we are all going be able to&nbsp;
enjoy this amazing paper soon. How cool is that?

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:33.060
So, does this get your mind going? What would you&nbsp;
use this for? Let me know in the comments below!

